WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.686
Output handling is a security boundary, not only presentation logic.

2
00:00:04.686 --> 00:00:19.708
Data that was harmless in one context can become unsafe in another context if it is placed into HTML, JavaScript, JSON, XML, CSV, templates, emails, logs, reports, or error pages without the right shaping and encoding.

3
00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:25.841
Razor and serializers provide helpful defaults, but unsafe contexts still require review.

4
00:00:25.841 --> 00:00:45.480
Raw HTML rendering, direct insertion into JavaScript, unsafe attribute construction, manually built JSON, manually built XML, CSV exports, document templates, and custom error pages can all create risk when untrusted data or sensitive internal fields are inserted without a clear rule.

5
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.512
Validation does not replace output encoding.

6
00:00:48.512 --> 00:00:54.025
A value can be valid for storage and still need encoding for the output context.

7
00:00:54.025 --> 00:00:57.746
Output encoding does not replace authorization either.

8
00:00:57.746 --> 00:01:03.396
A value that is correctly encoded may still be data the caller should not receive.

9
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:08.633
Domain models should not automatically become request or response contracts.

10
00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:19.659
Domain objects often contain internal flags, audit notes, administrative fields, tenant identifiers, navigation properties, secrets, and implementation details.

11
00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:25.861
Returning them directly can expose fields that were never intended for a caller or client.

12
00:01:25.861 --> 00:01:30.753
DTOs, response models, and view models help shape output intentionally.

13
00:01:30.753 --> 00:01:42.261
They let the application choose which fields are exposed, rename values for the contract, hide internal details, and avoid accidental serialization of related objects.

14
00:01:42.261 --> 00:01:46.464
They also make review easier because the boundary is visible.

15
00:01:46.464 --> 00:01:51.288
Serialization settings deserve review when sensitive data is involved.

16
00:01:51.288 --> 00:02:03.002
Polymorphic behavior, ignored fields, reference handling, custom converters, naming policies, and default value handling can change what enters or leaves the application.

17
00:02:03.002 --> 00:02:09.066
Treat output shape as part of the design, not a passive side effect of object structure.
